# meta-deploy.list: the queue meta-deploy.bash runs, in this order.
#
# STANDING RULE (CLAUDE/AgentNotes.md): this list is exactly what needs running NOW. Add a
# run when it is pending, remove it once it has run and is done, leave out what cannot
# usefully run yet, then ask the owner to run meta-deploy.bash. Every step a run needs lives
# in the plan's own scripts, so this is the only command the owner is handed. The order is by
# dependency. A plan with none of triage.bash, deploy.bash and acceptance.bash has nothing
# for the script to do. A triage-only plan is run once, read-only. Words after the folder
# name are passed to that plan's triage.bash, both runs.
#
# One entry per line, four kinds:
#   <plan folder>                  its triage, deploy and acceptance scripts, in order
#   playbooks/<path>.yml           a change no plan owns; run as one unit through its own
#                                  shebang, which goes through run.bash, exactly as
#                                  ./playbooks/imports/<play>.yml does by hand
#   <plan folder>/<script>.bash    one plan script, for a step none of the three fixed names
#                                  fits; run as one unit, like a playbook entry, except
#                                  that a script named *acceptance*.bash may exit 2 for
#                                  COULD NOT ESTABLISH, as an acceptance.bash may
#   changed-plays                  every play that has run here and has to run again, as
#                                  one unit: the checkout's ./run.bash --changed --yes, in
#                                  playbook-main.yml order, stopping at the first failure
#
# changed-plays runs WHERE IT STANDS in the list, like every other entry, and decides which
# plays it runs when it starts, so the entries above it have already run (a play an entry
# above ran is no longer changed, and is not run twice). Put it above the plan entries whose
# deploy or acceptance expects the changed plays to have run, which is the usual case, and
# below an entry it must follow. It replaces a hand-copied list of stale plays: name a
# playbooks/ entry only for a play that must run at a fixed point between two plan steps.
# Its description says which changed plays the agent expects it to pick up and why.
#
# A line starting with # is a comment and is the description of the entry below it: it is how
# the owner learns what a run will do, so write it. Blank lines are ignored. Any other line
# that is not an entry is a fatal error naming its line number.
# Plan 00185, colour emoji in terminals (#60), again: the last run's fc-cache ran as root
# under the cc session's umask 077 and left the system font cache 0600, so acceptance read a
# stale user cache (6 of 17 checks failed). triage.bash first (read-only; now lists the
# system cache files this user cannot read, and files other plays write as root that this
# user cannot read), then deploy.bash re-runs play-fonts.yml: Ansible now runs under umask
# 022 whatever shell started it (the umask_pin callback in ansible.cfg), and the play sees
# the unreadable system cache files and rebuilds the system cache and this user's own, which
# rewrites the damaged files 0644. Then acceptance.bash (18 checks: the 17 before, plus that
# every system cache file is readable), and triage again. It runs first so its triage sees
# the damage before anything repairs it. Close and reopen terminal windows afterwards; the
# issue's printf in a new window is left for Plan 00219.
00185-terminal-emoji-glyphs
# changed-plays: expected to pick up play-basic-configs.yml (yq and /etc/vimrc.local get an
# explicit 0755 and 0644, repairing them if a run under a restrictive umask left them
# root-only), plus any other play changed since it last ran here. play-fonts.yml has just
# run above, so it is not run again.
changed-plays
